---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Causal Inference in Statistical Genetics: Mendelian Randomization (MR)";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical and epidemiological exploration of Mendelian Randomization (MR): the three core instrumental variable assumptions, IVW regression, MR-Egger pleiotropy tests, the weighted median estimator, MVMR, and within-family sibling models.";
/* Derived from this page's own content: prose at 200 wpm, inline math priced by
   source length, and 15 s per display equation — the same model `lessonReadingTime`
   applies to the migrated lessons, so both renderers agree. Part 2 of the migration
   deletes this constant along with the page. */
const readingTime = "7 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 9</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Causal Inference</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      Observational epidemiological associations between clinical exposures (e.g. circulating biomarkers, lifestyle factors) and disease outcomes are notoriously plagued by <strong>unmeasured confounding</strong> and <strong>reverse causation</strong>. Can we use the random assortment of genetic alleles at conception as a natural randomized controlled trial to establish true causal relationships? <strong>Mendelian Randomization (MR)</strong> provides the mathematical framework for causal inference in genetics. This deep dive explores the <strong>three core Instrumental Variable (IV) assumptions</strong>, the <strong>Wald Ratio and Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) estimators</strong>, sensitivity diagnostics for <strong>horizontal pleiotropy (MR-Egger, Weighted Median, MR-PRESSO)</strong>, <strong>Multivariable MR (MVMR)</strong>, and <strong>within-family sibling MR</strong>.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#core-assumptions">1. The Three Core Instrumental Variable (IV) Assumptions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#estimators">2. Single-Instrument Wald Ratio & Multi-Instrument IVW Estimators</a></li>
        <li><a href="#weak-iv">3. Weak Instrument Bias & First-Stage F-Statistic</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pleiotropy">4. Horizontal Pleiotropy & MR-Egger Regression (Bowden et al. 2015)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#robust">5. Non-Parametric Robust Estimators: Weighted Median & MR-PRESSO</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mvmr">6. Multivariable MR (MVMR) & Disentangling Correlated Exposures</a></li>
        <li><a href="#within-family">7. Within-Family & Sibling MR: Eliminating Genetic Nurture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="core-assumptions">
      <h2>1. The Three Core Instrumental Variable (IV) Assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        Let <Katex tex="Z" /> be an inherited genetic variant (instrument), <Katex tex="X" /> be a modifiable phenotypic exposure (e.g. LDL cholesterol), <Katex tex="Y" /> be a health outcome (e.g. coronary artery disease), and <Katex tex="U" /> represent all unmeasured environmental and socioeconomic confounders.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Assumption</th>
              <th>Mathematical / Structural Definition</th>
              <th>Potential Violation & Diagnostic</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1. Relevance</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\operatorname{Cov}(Z, X) \ne 0" />: The variant is robustly associated with exposure <Katex tex="X" />.</td>
              <td><strong>Weak instrument bias:</strong> Diagnosed via first-stage <Katex tex="F\text{-statistic} > 10" />.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>2. Independence</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="Z \perp U" />: The variant is independent of all confounders of the <Katex tex="X \to Y" /> relationship.</td>
              <td><strong>Population stratification / Assortative mating:</strong> Controlled via PCA, LMMs, and within-family designs.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>3. Exclusion Restriction</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="Z" /> influences outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> <strong>solely and strictly through exposure <Katex tex="X" /></strong>.</td>
              <td><strong>Horizontal pleiotropy:</strong> Diagnosed via MR-Egger intercept, Weighted Median, and MR-PRESSO.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> Structural Formulation of Exclusion Restriction
        </div>
        <p>
          The Exclusion Restriction cannot be verified empirically through observed correlations alone. It is a <strong>structural assumption</strong> asserting that the direct path from <Katex tex="Z \to Y" /> is exactly zero when holding exposure <Katex tex="X" /> constant.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="estimators">
      <h2>2. Single-Instrument Wald Ratio & Multi-Instrument IVW Estimators</h2>

      <h3>1. The Wald Ratio Estimator</h3>
      <p>
        For a single genetic instrument <Katex tex="Z_j" />, let <Katex tex="\hat{\gamma}_j" /> be the estimated effect of <Katex tex="Z_j" /> on exposure <Katex tex="X" />, and <Katex tex="\hat{\Gamma}_j" /> be the estimated effect of <Katex tex="Z_j" /> on outcome <Katex tex="Y" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Single-Instrument Wald Ratio (Wald 1940)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j} = \frac{\hat{\Gamma}_j}{\hat{\gamma}_j}, \qquad \operatorname{SE}(\hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j}) \approx \sqrt{\frac{\sigma_Y^2}{\hat{\gamma}_j^2} + \frac{\hat{\Gamma}_j^2 \sigma_X^2}{\hat{\gamma}_j^4}} \approx \frac{\text{SE}(\hat{\Gamma}_j)}{|\hat{\gamma}_j|}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Derived using the <strong>Delta Method</strong> first-order Taylor series approximation.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Meta-Regression</h3>
      <p>
        When combining <Katex tex="K" /> independent instruments, the standard fixed-effects IVW causal estimator is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Multi-Instrument IVW Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{IVW}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j \hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{K} \hat{\gamma}_j \hat{\Gamma}_j \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Gamma}_j)^{-1}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} \hat{\gamma}_j^2 \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Gamma}_j)^{-1}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="w_j = \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j})^{-1} \approx \frac{\hat{\gamma}_j^2}{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Gamma}_j)}" />.
          <br/>
          IVW provides the maximum likelihood estimate of causal effect <Katex tex="\beta" /> when all <Katex tex="K" /> instruments are completely valid and exhibit zero horizontal pleiotropy.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="weak-iv">
      <h2>3. Weak Instrument Bias & First-Stage F-Statistic</h2>
      <p>
        If genetic instruments are weakly correlated with the exposure, the denominator <Katex tex="\hat{\gamma}_j" /> in the Wald ratio approaches zero, inducing severe finite-sample bias:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">First-Stage F-Statistic & Stock-Yogo Rule</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="F = \frac{\hat{\gamma}_j^2}{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\gamma}_j)} = \frac{R^2 (N - 1 - K)}{K (1 - R^2)} \approx \frac{N R^2}{K}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Stock-Yogo Guideline:</strong> <Katex tex="F > 10" /> ensures that the relative bias of the IV estimator compared to ordinary observational OLS is less than 10%. In modern two-sample MR, stringent instrument selection thresholds (<Katex tex="P < 5 \times 10^{-8} \implies F > 30" />) prevent weak instrument attenuation.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="pleiotropy">
      <h2>4. Horizontal Pleiotropy & MR-Egger Regression (Bowden et al. 2015)</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Horizontal Pleiotropy</strong> occurs when a genetic variant affects the outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> through an alternative biological pathway independent of exposure <Katex tex="X" />:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\Gamma}_j = \beta \hat{\gamma}_j + \alpha_j + \epsilon_j" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\alpha_j" /> is the direct pleiotropic effect of SNP <Katex tex="j" /> on <Katex tex="Y" />. If <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\alpha_j] \ne 0" /> (directional pleiotropy), standard IVW estimates are severely biased.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The InSIDE Assumption & MR-Egger Slope/Intercept</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Jack Bowden, George Davey Smith, and Stephen Burgess (2015)</strong> adapted Egger's meta-regression test to MR under the <strong>InSIDE assumption</strong> (Instrument Strength Independent of Direct Effect, meaning <Katex tex="\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{\gamma}_j, \alpha_j) = 0" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">MR-Egger Regression Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\Gamma}_j = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{Egger}} \hat{\gamma}_j + \epsilon_j" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          &bull; <strong>Egger Intercept (<Katex tex="\beta_0" />):</strong> Testing <Katex tex="H_0: \beta_0 = 0" /> provides a formal statistical test for directional horizontal pleiotropy.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Egger Slope (<Katex tex="\beta_{\text{Egger}}" />):</strong> Provides a consistent, unbiased estimate of the causal effect <Katex tex="\beta" /> even when directional pleiotropy is present!
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="robust">
      <h2>5. Non-Parametric Robust Estimators: Weighted Median & MR-PRESSO</h2>

      <h3>1. Weighted Median Estimator (Bowden et al. 2016)</h3>
      <p>
        Sorts the single-SNP Wald ratio estimates <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{(1)} \le \hat{\beta}_{(2)} \le \dots \le \hat{\beta}_{(K)}" /> with normalized weights <Katex tex="\tilde{w}_j = w_j / \sum w_k" />. The weighted median is the estimate at the 50th percentile of the cumulative weight distribution:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Weighted Median Estimator Robustness</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{WM}} = \text{median}\left(\hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j}, \tilde{w}_j\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>50% Breakdown Point:</strong> The Weighted Median estimator remains asymptotically consistent even if <strong>up to 50% of the genetic instrument weight comes from invalid / pleiotropic variants</strong>!
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2. MR-PRESSO (Verbanck et al. 2018)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>MR-Pleiotropy RESidual Sum and Outlier (MR-PRESSO)</strong> performs a global test of residual heterogeneity, identifies individual horizontal pleiotropic outlier variants via leave-one-out residual sums of squares, and recalculates outlier-corrected IVW causal estimates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="mvmr">
      <h2>6. Multivariable MR (MVMR) & Disentangling Correlated Exposures</h2>
      <p>
        When multiple risk factors share common genetic determinants (e.g. High-Density Lipoprotein [HDL], Low-Density Lipoprotein [LDL], and Triglycerides), standard univariable MR cannot distinguish which biomarker is genuinely causal.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Multivariable MR (MVMR, Sanderson et al. 2019)</strong> fits a joint multivariable regression of genetic variant effects across <Katex tex="D" /> exposures simultaneously:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Multivariable MR Causal Regression</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\Gamma}_j = \sum_{d=1}^{D} \beta_d \hat{\gamma}_{jd} + \epsilon_j" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\beta_d" /> estimates the <strong>direct causal effect</strong> of exposure <Katex tex="d" /> on outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> holding all other <Katex tex="D - 1" /> exposures constant.
          <br/>
          <em>Seminal Clinical Example:</em> MVMR proved that LDL-C and Apolipoprotein B (ApoB) are directly causal for cardiovascular disease, whereas HDL-C is non-causal (Voight et al. 2012).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="within-family">
      <h2>7. Within-Family & Sibling MR: Eliminating Genetic Nurture</h2>
      <p>
        Standard population-based GWAS and MR can be confounded by <strong>assortative mating</strong>, <strong>population stratification</strong>, and <strong>dynastic parental effects</strong> (where parental genotypes shape the child's environment, termed "genetic nurture").
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Within-Family / Sibling MR (Brumpton et al. 2020 Nature Genetics)</strong> analyzes phenotypic and genetic differences between full biological siblings:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Sibling Difference Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="(y_{i1} - y_{i2}) = \beta (x_{i1} - x_{i2}) + (\epsilon_{i1} - \epsilon_{i2})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Because meiotic segregation between siblings is strictly randomized conditional on parental genotypes, within-family MR provides the cleanest causal estimates in human genetics, completely invariant to population stratification and parental confounding.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Core IV Assumptions:</strong> Relevance (<Katex tex="F > 10" />), Independence (<Katex tex="Z \perp U" />), and Exclusion Restriction (<Katex tex="Z \to Y" /> strictly through <Katex tex="X" />).
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>IVW Regression:</strong> Combines independent Wald ratios (<Katex tex="\hat{\Gamma}_j / \hat{\gamma}_j" />) weighted by precision.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Pleiotropy Sensitivity:</strong> MR-Egger intercepts test directional pleiotropy; Weighted Median tolerates 50% invalid instruments.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>MVMR & Sibling MR:</strong> Disentangles correlated exposures and eliminates dynastic genetic nurture.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: The Modern Synthesis (Statistical Genetics Meets Deep Learning)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 10 to explore how regulatory sequence foundation models (Borzoi, AlphaGenome) and language models (GPN-Star) are bridging statistical associations to physical molecular mechanisms.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-deep-learning-synthesis/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 10: Deep Learning Synthesis</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
